{"step_index":81,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-18T13:37:23Z","content":"On recent versions of iOS, the native Camera app can sometimes fail to parse the `exp://` deep-link format from terminal ASCII codes.\n\nHere are the 2 fastest ways to open it on your iPhone:\n\n---\n\n### Option 1: Open via Safari on iPhone (Easiest)\n\n1. Look at your computer terminal output right under the QR code. You will see a line like:\n   ```text\n   Metro waiting on exp://192.168.x.x:8081\n   ```\n2. Open **Safari** on your iPhone.\n3. Type that exact address in Safari's address bar (e.g. `exp://192.168.1.45:8081` — replace with your computer's IP shown in the terminal).\n4. Tap **Go**. Safari will display a prompt:\n   > *\"Open in 'Expo Go'?\"*\n5. Tap **Open**.\n\n---\n\n### Option 2: Enter URL Manually in Expo Go\n\n1. Open the **Expo Go** app on your iPhone.\n2. Under the development section, tap **\"Enter URL manually\"**.\n3. Type the address from your terminal (e.g. `exp://192.168.x.x:8081`).\n4. Tap **Connect**.\n\n---\n\n### Option 3: Use the Web Browser QR Code\n\nIn your terminal while Expo is running:\n1. Press **`w`** on your keyboard (or open `http://localhost:8081` in Chrome/Edge on your PC).\n2. The browser renders a high-definition graphic QR code on screen.\n3. Point your iPhone Camera at the high-definition QR code on your computer monitor.","thinking":"**Analyzing QR Code Errors**\n\nI've hit a snag. The user's iPhone, when scanning the QR code, produced \"no usable data found.\" I'm diving into why this might happen with Expo on iOS. Specifically, it seems the native iOS Camera app requires either an `exp://<ip>:<port>` or `http://` link format to work correctly, which is a key lead.\n\n\n"}
