{"step_index":117,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-18T17:54:03Z","content":"This \"taking an excessively long time to load\" timeout happens on Windows when **Expo advertises a secondary virtual network adapter IP** (from WSL, Docker, Hyper-V, or VirtualBox) instead of your computer's actual Wi-Fi IP. The iPhone tries to download the bundle from the wrong internal address and times out.\n\nHere is how to fix it in 60 seconds:\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Find Your Computer's Real Wi-Fi IP\n\n1. Open PowerShell or Command Prompt on your PC and run:\n   ```powershell\n   ipconfig\n   ```\n2. Look for **Wireless LAN adapter Wi-Fi** (or Ethernet if plugged in) and find the **IPv4 Address** (for example: `192.168.1.45` or `10.0.0.12`).\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Force Expo to Use That Exact Wi-Fi IP\n\nIn your terminal in PowerShell, run:\n\n```powershell\n$env:REACT_NATIVE_PACKAGER_HOSTNAME=\"YOUR_WIFI_IPV4\"\nnpx expo start -c\n```\n*(Replace `YOUR_WIFI_IPV4` with your real IP from Step 1, e.g. `$env:REACT_NATIVE_PACKAGER_HOSTNAME=\"192.168.1.45\"`).*\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Open in Expo Go on iPhone\n\n1. Scan the new QR code with your iPhone Camera (or select the project in Expo Go).\n2. You will see the terminal instantly show:\n   ```text\n   iOS Bundled 100% (index.js)\n   ```\n3. The app will open in a few seconds on your phone screen!","thinking":"**Investigating Expo Go Load**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on the frustrating Expo Go loading issue. It appears users, after scanning a QR code or syncing accounts, are met with an excessively long loading time, ultimately leading to failure on their iPhones. The crux of my thought process is: why is Metro bundler timing out or taking an age to produce that initial iOS bundle on Windows?\n\n\n"}
